.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.\" Copyright (C) 2026 Kevin H. Patterson
.Dd November 5, 2026
.Dt REZAR 1
.Os zar @PROJECT_VERSION@
.Sh NAME
.Nm rezar
.Nd add, replace, or delete paths in a zar archive
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Ar options
.Fl o Ar output
.Ar archive
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
writes a new archive,
.Ar output :
a copy of
.Ar archive ,
made by
.Xr zar 1 ,
with paths added, replaced, or deleted.
Everything kept is copied block by block, never decompressed or
recompressed, so editing a large archive takes about as long as copying
it.
Blocks only deleted files used are left out, so their space is reclaimed.
Blocks that several files share stay shared.
.Ar archive
itself is never changed, and
.Ar output
can't be the same file.
.Pp
The paths to change are given in lists, one path per line
.Pq or, with Fl 0 , No separated by NUL bytes ,
or one at a time with options such as
.Fl \-add .
Each is a path in the archive.
What is added there is read from the same path below the directory given by
.Fl C :
.Bl -tag -width "a/d/**" -offset indent
.It Pa a/b
the file or symbolic link
.Pa a/b
(not followed)
.It Pa a/d/
the directory
.Pa a/d
alone; deleted only if it ends up empty
.It Pa a/d/**
the directory
.Pa a/d
and everything below it
.El
.Pp
All deletions happen first, then all additions, so the order of the lines
doesn't matter, but a path may be listed only once in all.
Missing parent directories of what's added are added too.
A hard link to a file that is deleted or replaced keeps the old content,
as an ordinary file.
If anything listed is wrong, such as a path to add that is already in the
archive, every such problem is reported and nothing is written.
.Pp
New files are stored as
.Xr zar 1
would store them, compressed with the archive's dictionary if it has one.
Entries are written in path order.
A solid archive
.Pq Nm zar Fl \-solid
can't be edited, since everything in it would have to be recompressed.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl o , Fl \-output Ns = Ns Ar file
Write the new archive to
.Ar file .
It is written to a temporary file and renamed into place only when
complete.
.It Fl y , Fl \-yes
Overwrite
.Ar output
if it exists.
.It Fl C , Fl \-directory Ns = Ns Ar dir
Read the paths to add from below
.Ar dir .
The default is the current directory.
.It Fl \-add-list Ns = Ns Ar file
Add the paths listed in
.Ar file ,
or standard input if
.Ar file
is
.Ql - .
They must not be in the archive yet.
.It Fl \-replace-list Ns = Ns Ar file
Replace the paths listed, which must be in the archive.
Replacing
.Pa a/d/**
replaces the whole directory with what is in
.Ar dir
now.
.It Fl \-add-replace-list Ns = Ns Ar file
Add the paths listed, replacing any that are already in the archive.
.It Fl \-delete-list Ns = Ns Ar file
Delete the paths listed, which must be in the archive.
.It Fl \-add Ns = Ns Ar path , Fl \-replace Ns = Ns Ar path , Fl \-add-replace Ns = Ns Ar path , Fl \-delete Ns = Ns Ar path
The same, for one
.Ar path .
Each may be given more than once, and they combine with the lists.
.It Fl 0 , Fl \-null
Lists are separated by NUL bytes rather than newlines, as from
.Ql find -print0 ,
so that paths may contain newlines.
.It Fl n , Fl \-dry-run
Print each entry that would be removed, after a
.Ql - ,
and each path that would be added, after a
.Ql + ,
and write nothing.
.It Fl \-dedup Ns = Ns Cm on Ns | Ns Cm off Ns | Ns Cm deep
Store identical chunks of new files only once:
.Cm on ,
the default.
.Cm deep
also reuses blocks already in the archive that hold the same data,
decompressing those of the same length to compare them.
.It Fl \-align Ns Op = Ns Ar n
Align block payloads to
.Ar n
bytes, a power of two up to 1M; without
.Ar n ,
the CPU's cache-line size.
The default is the archive's alignment.
.It Fl \-head-index Ns Op = Ns Cm on Ns | Ns Cm off
Also write the index at the front of the archive, so that
.Xr unzar 1
can extract it from a pipe.
The default is to do so if
.Ar archive
has one;
.Cm on
without a value.
.It Fl \-verify Ns = Ns Ar pubfile
First check that
.Ar archive
is signed by the public key in
.Ar pubfile ;
may be given more than once, to accept any of several keys.
.It Fl \-hash , Fl \-sign Ns = Ns Ar keyfile , Fl \-sequence Ns = Ns Ar n , Fl \-name Ns = Ns Ar text
Hash and sign the new archive, as in
.Xr zar 1 .
The hashes
.Ar archive
already has are checked as each block is copied, and kept, and if it has any,
what is added is hashed too.
Its signature is not kept, since the new archive has a new root: sign it
again with
.Fl \-sign .
.It Fl v , Fl \-verbose
List each path on standard error as it is added, and print a summary at the
end.
.It Fl \-help
Show a summary of the options.
.It Fl \-version
Show the version, the archive format version, and the zstd version.
.El
.Pp
These options apply to the files added, as in
.Xr zar 1 :
.Fl l , Fl \-level ,
.Fl j , Fl \-threads ,
.Fl \-chunk-size ,
.Fl \-owner ,
.Fl \-nsec ,
.Fl \-all-times ,
.Fl \-names ,
.Fl \-compress-all ,
and
.Fl \-contiguous .
.Pp
Short options can be combined, as in
.Fl yv0 .
The count for
.Fl j
must be attached, as in
.Fl j4 .
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
Success.
.It 1
The archive was written, but some files had problems, each reported on
standard error.
.It 2
A fatal error: bad usage, a mistake in the lists, an unreadable or solid
archive, an existing output without
.Fl y ,
and so on.
No archive is left behind.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Replace one file and add a directory, read from
.Pa ~/src :
.Dl $ rezar -C ~/src --replace=proj/main.c --add='proj/docs/**' -o new.zar proj.zar
.Pp
Delete everything listed in
.Pa gone.txt ,
after checking what that would do:
.Dl $ rezar -n --delete-list=gone.txt proj.zar
.Dl $ rezar --delete-list=gone.txt -o trimmed.zar proj.zar
.Pp
Delete the
.Pa .o
files that
.Xr find 1
finds in the tree the archive was made from, whatever characters their
names hold:
.Dl $ (cd ~/src && find proj -name '*.o' -print0) | rezar -0 --delete-list=- -o clean.zar proj.zar
.Pp
Add a page-aligned head index to an archive, changing nothing else:
.Dl $ rezar --align=4K --head-index -o aligned.zar proj.zar
.Pp
Quote paths ending in
.Ql /**
on the command line, or the shell may expand them.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr unzar 1 ,
.Xr zar 1
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Kevin H. Patterson
